+++ b/domains/findings/pages/nothing-compiles-the-route-files-importing-generated-route-types.finding.ts
@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const nothingCompilesTheRouteFilesImportingGeneratedRouteTypes = {
   claim:
     "112 route files import react-router's generated `+types` declarations, and nothing compiles them. `typecheck` excludes them and records a `gap` saying each app's own compile judges them, and no app compile runs anywhere: there is no CI, the root tsconfig states an empty file list and no references, per-app typecheck scripts exist that nothing invokes, and deploy builds through Vite, which strips types without checking them. `alan/atlas-web` and `alan/web-capacitor` hold no tsconfig at all, so 16 of the 112 cannot be compiled even by hand. `typecheck` cannot take them either: react-router's route types hang off one global `Register` interface that seven apps augment incompatibly, so one program over the tree types other apps' params as an empty object or, where a route name collides, clean against the wrong app's params.",
   evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-05, at HEAD after b23007e36b, in a scratch harness under `/var/tmp` importing the check's own exported pieces, so the program built is the check's program rather than a lookalike.\n\nTHE POPULATION. 112 files, taken from `git ls-files` filtered to `.ts` and `.tsx` importers of a `+types` segment, dropping `dist/` and the fixture and finding files carrying the literal in prose. By app: `alan/web` 41, `temper/temper-web` 33, `alan/atlas-web` 16, `archive-of-worlds/archive-of-worlds-web` 10, `smilingjenny/smilingjenny-web` 9, `audhdalan/audhdalan-web` 3. `akasha typecheck` over all 112 exits 3 with 112 refusals, each reading `was not judged — nothing in the tree holds the route types it imports`, emitted at `commands/typecheck/typecheck.command.code.ts:162`.\n\nPRESENCE IS NOT THE OBSTACLE. The generated declaration is on disk for 112 of 112, 0 missing, so a refusal telling someone to run typegen would buy nothing. Serving them to the check's program through an overlay mapping `<app>/<rel>/+types/<name>.ts` onto `<app>/.react-router/types/<rel>/+types/<name>.ts` resolves every one: 0 TS2307. It also draws 59 diagnostics across 28 files, 46 TS2345 and 13 TS2322, and all 59 are false.\n\nWHY THEY ARE FALSE. The generated declarations derive params through `GetInfo`, which reads a global augmentation of react-router's `Register` interface declared in each app's own `+routes.ts`. Without that file in the program every route's params degrade to `string | undefined`, which is the literal shape of 58 of the 59. Three variants measured: no `+routes.ts` draws 59 false diagnostics; `alan/web`'s alone leaves that app clean and gives the others 9 TS2339 reading `Property does not exist on type '{}'`; all seven together give 18 TS2717, six apps colliding on three properties of the one `Register`.\n\nWHY SCOPING PER APP DOES NOT ESCAPE IT. `reachedBy` reaches importers transitively, and a run over route files alone produced 122 roots spanning six apps. A change to a shared module therefore draws several apps' routes into one program, and whichever route map is installed types the rest as an empty object — or, where a route name collides across apps, types them clean against the wrong app's params. That is a silent false green rather than a refusal, which is the worst thing a check can answer.\n\nBOTH NUMBERS. Under the naive overlay: 84 of 112 clean, 28 files carrying 59 diagnostics, 0 of the 59 genuine. Under each app's own correct route map: 112 of 112 clean, 0 genuinely red, with a fault seeded per app and the instrument reporting 112 seen and 0 blind, so that zero is a clean tree rather than a blind run.\n\nADJACENT. `checks/cluster-checks/modules/unbuilt-router-apps/unbuilt-router-apps.module.code.ts:15-24` treats any workspace stating a build script as built, so all seven apps are excluded and `rr-server-module-in-client` examines nothing, while its own header says no CI step runs a production build for these apps.\n\nWHAT WOULD JUDGE IT. A per-app compile running `react-router typegen` and then the app's own tsconfig. `rootDirs` and the `Register` augmentation are per-app by construction, so a per-app program is the only program that can hold them. It is blocked first on `alan/atlas-web` and `alan/web-capacitor`, which need a tsconfig before anything can compile them.\n\nNOT MEASURED. I did not establish what a per-app compile would cost on every change, nor whether the two apps holding no tsconfig are built at all or compile through `alan/web`. Nobody has read what either app builds, and a wrong tsconfig would read as coverage that is not there.",
+    "Measured 2026-09-05, at HEAD after b23007e36b, in a scratch harness under `/var/tmp` importing the check's own exported pieces, so the program built is the check's program rather than a lookalike.\n\nTHE POPULATION. 112 files, taken from `git ls-files` filtered to `.ts` and `.tsx` importers of a `+types` segment, dropping `dist/` and the fixture and finding files carrying the literal in prose. By app: `alan/web` 41, `temper/temper-web` 33, `alan/atlas-web` 16, `archive-of-worlds/archive-of-worlds-web` 10, `smilingjenny/smilingjenny-web` 9, `audhdalan/audhdalan-web` 3. `akasha typecheck` over all 112 exits 3 with 112 refusals, each reading `was not judged — nothing in the tree holds the route types it imports`, emitted at `command-system/commands/typecheck/typecheck.command.code.ts:162`.\n\nPRESENCE IS NOT THE OBSTACLE. The generated declaration is on disk for 112 of 112, 0 missing, so a refusal telling someone to run typegen would buy nothing. Serving them to the check's program through an overlay mapping `<app>/<rel>/+types/<name>.ts` onto `<app>/.react-router/types/<rel>/+types/<name>.ts` resolves every one: 0 TS2307. It also draws 59 diagnostics across 28 files, 46 TS2345 and 13 TS2322, and all 59 are false.\n\nWHY THEY ARE FALSE. The generated declarations derive params through `GetInfo`, which reads a global augmentation of react-router's `Register` interface declared in each app's own `+routes.ts`. Without that file in the program every route's params degrade to `string | undefined`, which is the literal shape of 58 of the 59. Three variants measured: no `+routes.ts` draws 59 false diagnostics; `alan/web`'s alone leaves that app clean and gives the others 9 TS2339 reading `Property does not exist on type '{}'`; all seven together give 18 TS2717, six apps colliding on three properties of the one `Register`.\n\nWHY SCOPING PER APP DOES NOT ESCAPE IT. `reachedBy` reaches importers transitively, and a run over route files alone produced 122 roots spanning six apps. A change to a shared module therefore draws several apps' routes into one program, and whichever route map is installed types the rest as an empty object — or, where a route name collides across apps, types them clean against the wrong app's params. That is a silent false green rather than a refusal, which is the worst thing a check can answer.\n\nBOTH NUMBERS. Under the naive overlay: 84 of 112 clean, 28 files carrying 59 diagnostics, 0 of the 59 genuine. Under each app's own correct route map: 112 of 112 clean, 0 genuinely red, with a fault seeded per app and the instrument reporting 112 seen and 0 blind, so that zero is a clean tree rather than a blind run.\n\nADJACENT. `checks/cluster-checks/modules/unbuilt-router-apps/unbuilt-router-apps.module.code.ts:15-24` treats any workspace stating a build script as built, so all seven apps are excluded and `rr-server-module-in-client` examines nothing, while its own header says no CI step runs a production build for these apps.\n\nWHAT WOULD JUDGE IT. A per-app compile running `react-router typegen` and then the app's own tsconfig. `rootDirs` and the `Register` augmentation are per-app by construction, so a per-app program is the only program that can hold them. It is blocked first on `alan/atlas-web` and `alan/web-capacitor`, which need a tsconfig before anything can compile them.\n\nNOT MEASURED. I did not establish what a per-app compile would cost on every change, nor whether the two apps holding no tsconfig are built at all or compile through `alan/web`. Nobody has read what either app builds, and a wrong tsconfig would read as coverage that is not there.",
 } as const satisfies Finding
